  "lower_bound_explanation": "A and B can each pair only with H; F and G can each pair only with N. Thus at least two up cells remain uncovered. Every blue covers one up and one down cell, and the board starts with seven of each, so the number of uncovered down cells equals the number of uncovered up cells. At least four green fillers are therefore necessary; the five-blue packing attains four.",
  "generalization": "For a balanced region with n up and n down cells, any up subset S with |S|-|N(S)|=d forces at least d up gaps and d down gaps, hence at least 2d green fillers. The exact minimum is 2 times the maximum such deficit (including the empty subset). This last equality is the deficiency form of Hall's matching theorem; the elementary subset argument alone proves the lower bound, not its attainability."
